;; For consumers that use the Clojure CLI: `{:local/root "../replique-cljs"}` puts
;; the source on their classpath directly, with no `mvn install` step in between,
;; so an edit here is picked up by their next REPL.
;;
;; The Maven build (pom.xml) is the one that runs the tests and builds a jar; this
;; file exists only so tools.deps can see the source tree.
;;
;; CLOJURE IS NOT NAMED HERE, deliberately. The compiler runs on replique-clj, and
;; it is the CONSUMER that must name it, because it stands in for
;; org.clojure/clojure - which the Clojure CLI's root deps.edn always puts on the
;; classpath, and which only the consuming project can override.
{;; THE ONE LIBRARY DEPENDENCY, and it is a ClojureScript one rather than a JVM
 ;; one: nothing in this compiler calls it. cljs/reader.cljs is vendored beside
 ;; cljs/core.cljs and opens (:require [cljs.tools.reader :as treader]
 ;; [cljs.tools.reader.edn :as edn]), so the seven cljs/tools/reader*.cljs files in
 ;; this jar are sources the COMPILER has to be able to find for a program that
 ;; reads edn - and clojure/edn.cljs, which is three delegating functions over
 ;; cljs.reader, is the name most programs reach for.
 ;;
 ;; Named here as well as in pom.xml, and that is the point: a consumer arriving by
 ;; :local/root gets :paths and :deps out of this file and never reads the pom, so
 ;; a pom-only declaration would leave cljs.reader shipped and unloadable on
 ;; exactly the path replique uses.
 ;;
 ;; 1.3.6 is the version ClojureScript 1.12.145 pins, for the same reason the
 ;; google-closure-library version is that one: the vendored cljs/reader.cljs was
 ;; written against it.
 :deps {org.clojure/tools.reader {:mvn/version "1.3.6"}}

 ;; src/clj FIRST, and target/classes only for what is not in it: the websocket
 ;; framing under clojure.cljs.ws is Java (src/java), so the only build of it is
 ;; Maven's and a consumer reading this source tree has nowhere else to find it -
 ;; without it clojure.cljs.browser will not load and a browser REPL is missing.
 ;; Maven also copies the .clj sources in there, which is why the order matters:
 ;; the live ones shadow the copies rather than the other way round.
 :paths ["src/clj" "target/classes"]}
